.TH SSH 1
.SH NAME
ssh \- the SSH client: a remote shell or command
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ssh
.RB [ \-p
.IR port ]
.RB [ \-i
.IR key ]
.RI [ user @] host
.RI [ command ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B ssh
connects to
.I host
over SSH-2 (curve25519-sha256, an ssh-ed25519 host key, then
chacha20-poly1305@openssh.com: OpenSSH's default algorithms) and runs a
shell there, or
.I command
when one is given.
.I user
is your own name unless said.
.PP
On the first connection to a host, its key's fingerprint
.RB ( SHA256:... ,
as OpenSSH shows it) is shown with
"Are you sure you want to continue connecting (yes/no)?";
.B yes
adds it to
.I ~/.ssh/known_hosts
.RI ( host
for port 22,
.RI [ host ]: port
otherwise, OpenSSH's format). A host whose key changed is refused, with its
new fingerprint and the line to remove.
.PP
Authentication: the key
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
(or the one of
.BR \-i )
if the server takes it, else a password, read without echo (three tries).
.PP
A shell gets a terminal on the server (80 x 24) and the console goes raw:
every key goes to the server as typed, and the server echoes.
.B ~.
after Enter disconnects. A command gets no terminal and no input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-p " port"
The server's port (22 by default).
.TP
.BI \-i " key"
The private key file to authenticate with.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The remote command's (or shell's) status; 255 for ssh's own errors.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/id_ed25519
Your private key (made by
.BR ssh-keygen (1)).
.TP
.I ~/.ssh/known_hosts
The keys of the hosts you accepted.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
ssh alice@10.0.2.2
ssh \-p 2222 10.0.2.2 uname \-a
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ssh-keygen (1),
.BR sshd (8)
.SH BUGS
One channel per connection: no port forwarding, agent, X11, scp or sftp.
No re-keying: OpenSSH asks for new keys after 1 GiB each way, and the
connection then ends. Ed25519 keys only, unencrypted key files only (one
with a passphrase is reported and skipped). The remote terminal is
80 x 24 whatever the window. A session ended by a signal reports status
255.
